now EU energy ministers are meeting in  Prague to find ways to tackle high gas  prices 15 members of the European Union  want the price of wholesale gas to be  capped across the block they do not want  richer countries in Europe to be able to  buy gas at higher prices  now the uh they argue that the cap would  help curb prices before expensive gas  supplies enter the market reach power  plants and therefore drive up  electricity bills The Proposal is based  on three ideas setting a limit on the  cost of Russian gas Imports negotiating  a lower gas price with other suppliers  and establishing a ceiling on the price  of gas used to generate electricity but  some EU member states disagree with the  plan and they want alternative options  including a limit on gas consumption and  subsidy packages for businesses and for  consumers  Sebastian is at that meeting in Prague  and joins me now so we just outlined  step that there are different views on  how to deal with this right how to  reduce gas prices in Europe  how deep are those divisions  well they are very deep but it's a new  day and a new meeting here in Prague and  also New Hope that they will come to an  agreement but last week's leaders Summit  here in Prague really failed to reach  that agreement and even worse it showed  the real deep divisions between the  different countries to 27 member states  especially between the so-called rich or  north and west maybe and the more poor  east and south they really have a  different view on how to resolve this  crisis the the more richer countries  really don't want any Market  intervention or at least try to limit it  as much as possible but at the same time  the handing out really large packages  for their own people to uh to to deal  with these high energy prices something  the the countries in the south and east  simply can't afford so this is where the  divisions are really at their deepest  and they have had meetings also last  night they've had meetings trying to  come to some kind of agreement I spoke  to the minister of industry and trade  here in the Czech Republic who is  organizing this meeting and he really  called on the countries to unite and to  show some solidarity because of course  amidst this whole war in Ukraine going  on what the EU wants least is to show  this Unity to show conflict they want to  show that they're strong against Russia  but this whole energy crisis is simply  showing something else and and your it  is true that the European Union  countries have been able to put up a  united front by and large when it comes  to the the Russia Ukraine crisis do you  think they'll end up agreeing on a price  cap  well that's a very good question uh the  the minister of industry and trade was  kind of optimistic they are discussing a  so-called compromise solution what he  really aims at now is to put a cap on a  gas for electricity so basically a lot  of countries are still using gas to  generate power and that really leads to  high bills for consumers so if they can  put a cap or they call it decoupling the  price from the gas price then that would  help a lot of consumers already it would  help a lot of countries but the same  system has been used since June in Spain  and Portugal and it has reduced prices  by nearly 25 percent but what it also  has done is it has increased consumption  and that's something that the EU  definitely wants to avoid because  they're really worried that they simply  will run out and there will be blackouts  and that is a real worry especially in  the countries like Germany that use a  lot of energy so it is is a discussion  that will be ongoing today and also the  Netherlands and Germany have put in  their own proposal still trying to avoid  this price Gap they're looking at a new  Benchmark price for LNG that's something  that's being discussed and they also  really push for a joint buying effort of  gas on the market for the whole block so  we don't expect any resolution today  what they're aiming for is now to reach  a compromise to reach a solution early  November but of course winter is already  kicking in here and people are really  worried with their bills now step listen  thank you very much for that explanation  because it can be hard to understand  exactly the mechanism how you get to  reduce the price of gas but the bottom  line is something very simple reducing  energy bills for consumers in Europe  Steph fasten thank you very much
 

